## MAIN TEXT
## INTRODUCTION
The hetero-epitaxial growth1-2 of metal-organic frameworks3-6 (MOFs) is a convenient way to integrate MOFs as active materials into devices7-9 or nano/microsystems,10-13 which, not only demonstrates the advantage of material hybridization in the original applications13-18 of MOFs, but also provides a route for the development of sensors,19-20 thermoelectrics,21-22 photovoltaics,23-24 and electrocatalysts.25-27 Although liquid-phase epitaxy is one of most frequently employed synthetic strategies for the growth of MOFs on a given surface,9, 28 direct epitaxial growth is limited only to a very few MOFs 29 on specific substrates with appropriate lattice matching30 because of the anisotropic growth properties31 of MOFs. In most cases, in order to obtain substrate-supported MOF films or core-shell structures with MOFs as shells, it is necessary to functionalize the surface with terminal organic groups32-34, which can anchor the metal nodes and then the organic ligands, leading to surface-attached nucleation and growth23, 35-36 of MOFs. Unfortunately, metal nodes for construction of MOFs are limited9, 28 only to the paddle-wheel structural metal linkers using this approach. Therefore, the development of a liquid-phase epitaxy approach, suitable for MOFs of different types, is required.
Recently, we reported37-39 a class of environmentally benign and porous extended frameworks—namely CD-MOFs—which are constructed from alkali metal salts and $\gamma$ -CD. CD-MOF crystals have cubic morphologies with body-centered cubic unit cells, constructed of nine $(\gamma\mathrm{-CD)_{6}}$ cubes, each face of which is composed of a $\gamma$ -CD torus. The frameworks of CD-MOFs with similar extended structures can be constructed using different alkali metal ions, e.g., $\mathrm{Li}^{+39}\mathrm{K}^{+37}$ or ${\mathrm{Rb}}^{+}$ ,38 none of which are paddle-wheel-like metal linkers. CD-MOFs are versatile reticular materials for use in molecular recognition, selective adsorption and separation40-41 as a result of their porous homochiral frameworks. More importantly, CD-MOFs exhibit large proton conductivities42 which experience significant decrease43-44 on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ infusion.
Despite the obvious attractions of CD-MOFs, it remains challenging to grow them epitaxially on a given substrate, an impediment which limits the development of potential applications of CD-MOFs.
Here, we demonstrate the hetero-epitaxial growth of CD-MOFs on surfaces covered with self-assembled monolayers (SAMs) of organic ligands (γ-CD). These SAMs can be deposited by anchoring $\gamma$ -CD tori based on host-guest complexation45-46 with the chemically modified pyrene units attached to the surfaces. Employing liquid-phase epitaxy, in combination with this host-guest strategy, (i) substrate-supported CD-MOFs films have been prepared and (ii) the films shown to possess polycrystalline morphologies with an out-of-plane $c$ -axis) structural orientation, along with continuous areas produced on the $\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ scale. Furthermore, this strategy has also been employed in the growth of CD-MOFs on the curved surfaces of silica microspheres as the core and CD-MOFs as the shell. Finally, we demonstrate the fabrication of an electronic sensor device, based on the integration of MOF films using the liquid-phase epitaxy approach with this host-guest strategy: this devices exhibit an approximately three-second response to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and results in a 50-fold decrease in conductivity which is recovered within 10 seconds after re-exposing the device to air.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The substrate with SAMs of $\gamma$ -CD on the surface was designed for the epitaxial growth of CD-MOFs. Specifically, glass slides were used (Scheme 1) as representative flat substrates. The exposed silanol groups on the glass surface were first of all reacted (Scheme 1a, Step 1) with an appropriate amino silanol alkoxide, and then the surface-grafted amino groups were condensed with 1-pyrenecarboxyaldehyde, followed by reductive amination (Scheme 1a, Step 2). The contact angle of water on the glass slides changed (Figure S1a, b) from ${\sim}20^{\circ}$ to ${\sim}59^{\circ}$ after functional group grafting, an observation indicating that the surface becomes more hydrophobic as a result of the pyrene groups functionalization (Scheme 1b). By immersion of the pyrene-functionalized substrates into an aqueous solution of $\gamma$ -CD for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the water contact angle fell (Figure S1c) from ${\sim}59^{\circ}$ to ${\sim}4^{\circ}$ , suggesting that the conversion of the hydrophobic surface to a hydrophilic one, on account of the host-guest inclusion of the pyrenes by the hydrophilic $\gamma$ -CD tori on the surface (Scheme 1c), had taken place.
Surface-attached CD-MOF films were prepared by immersing the glass slides, which were modified with SAMs of $\gamma$ -CD, in an aqueous solution of $\gamma$ -CD and ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{CO}}_{3}$ , followed by vapor diffusion of MeOH into the aqueous solution. In order to investigate the nucleation and growth process of CD-MOFs on the surface, the glass slides were monitored by optical microscopy over the course of several days. Islet rectangles of different sizes and random in-plane orientations were observed (Figure 1a) after three days, revealing the non-uniform nucleation of CD-MOF crystals which are known from previous reports37-39 to be cubic on this particular substrate. Here we observe, however, only two-dimensional rectangular sheets with four sharp edges on the substrate instead of any three-dimensional shapes in the microscopic images (top view), suggesting that these CD-MOF crystals are oriented in the out-of-plane ( $\dot{}c$ -axis) direction. Upon diffusing solvent for an additional day, the CD-MOF crystals were observed to grow into larger areas, forming sheets that begin (Figure 1b) to fuse with other adjacent crystals. In comparison with the images taken on the third day, the edges of crystals were still very sharp with $90^{\circ}$ angles at the corners in top-view images, an observation which suggests again that CD-MOFs are growing on the substrate with an out-of-plane orientation. Following solvent diffusion for five days, the surfaces of the glass supports were found to be covered completely with CD-MOFs to the extent that large areas of continuous CD-MOF sheets could be observed by optical microscopy (Figure 1c), indicating the successful growth of the surface-attached CD-MOF polycrystalline film on the glass slide decorated with SAMs of $\gamma$ -CD tori. Both top (Figure 1d) and cross-section (Figure 1e) images, recorded by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), indicate that the film is composed of close-packed and confluent-growth crystals with out-of-plane orientations. Further inspection of these SEM images indicates that the films consist of two-to-three layers of sheet-shaped CD-MOF crystals with a total thickness of ${\sim}2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ . Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) mapping images confirm (Figure S2) the homogeneous distribution of elemental carbon, oxygen and potassium. The out-of-plane X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the substrate-supported film exhibit (Figure. 1f) strong peaks at $2\theta$ $=5.6$ and $11.2^{\circ}$ which correspond to the (200) and (400) Bragg diffractions planes of the CD-MOF crystal, respectively. In contrast, the powder XRD pattern of the CD-MOF stripped from the glass substrates shows a strong peak at $2\theta=3.9~^{\circ}$ , indexed as the (101) plane, and a weak peak at $5.6^{\circ}$ , indexed as the (200) plane, also demonstrating that the CD-MOF crystals are oriented with the (100) lattice plane parallel to the substrates. In addition, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of the stripped crystalline CD-MOF powders establishes (Figure S3) the fact that their frameworks are thermally stable up to $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in an inert atmosphere.
Gas adsorption−desorption measurements (Figure S4) were performed on the powders which were scraped off from the oriented CD-MOF polycrystalline films on glass substrates. The $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherm at $77~\mathrm{K}$ shows the representative type-I curve (Figure S4a), wherein only one capillary condensation step was observed at $P/P_{\theta}=0–0.1$ , an observation which is characteristic of a highly microporous material. The pore sizes in the CD-MOF film (Figure S4b) are found to be $\mathrm{\sim}1~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter by simulating the adsorption data using density functional theory (DFT) and Monte-Carlo methods47. On the basis of $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms, the Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface area of the CD-MOF films was calculated (Figure S4c) to be $1220~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption isotherms for CD-MOF films indicate (Figure S4d) $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacities of $4.89\mathrm{~mmol~g}^{-1}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ , and $3.8~\mathrm{mmol~g}^{-1}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively. In addition, the $\Nu_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption measurements for the activated crystalline CD-MOF powders indicates (Figure S5) they have an almost identical porosity as the film crystals. All of these data establish the accessible porosity of these CD-MOF films.
In order to clarify the effect of the $\gamma$ -CD functionalized substrate surface on the growth of CD-MOF films, glass slices without/with different surface modifications were also used as substrates to grow CD-MOF film under the same conditions. After solvent diffusion for 5 days, the formation of only a few cubic crystals with sizes of ${\sim}100~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ attached to the glass slices at various different angles was observed for the unmodified ones (Figure S6a) and also those functionalized with amine groups (Figure S6b). The growth of discrete cubic CD-MOF crystals reveals their anisotropic growth properties,48 suggesting the lack of the surface-attached nucleation in the absence of the SAMs of $\gamma$ -CD. Meanwhile, the CD-MOF crystals grow with random orientations in all axes on the non-interacting substrates, suggesting that the unmodified glass slides cannot direct the growth of CD-MOF crystals in a specific orientation. When using the glass slides modified with pyrene groups as substrates, surface-attached CD-MOF films in low quality can be obtained (Figure S6c), an observation which indicating that the pretreatment of immersing in aqueous solution of $\gamma$ -CD is an important step the formation of SAMs of $\gamma$ -CD.
To obtain further insight into the hetero-epitaxial growth properties of CD-MOF, silica microspheres with sizes of ${\sim}1.3~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , which were chosen (Figure 2) as curved surfaces for growing CD-MOFs, were synthesized by the Stöber method49 (Figure 2a, 2e) and modified (Figure 2b) with SAMs of $\gamma$ -CD. After growing (Figure 2c-d) CD-MOFs employing the same solvent-diffusion protocol, the derivatized microparticles were characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM). The silica microspheres were found (Figure 2e) to be coated with the cubic nanocrystals of ${\sim}200\ \mathrm{nm}$ in size, demonstrating the formation of the core-shell structure wherein silica microsphere is the core and the CD-MOF polycrystals constitute the shell. When using the silica microparticles without surface functionalization (Figure S7), CD-MOF crystals cannot be grown as the shell coating on the particles. All these observations indicate that the derivatization of the substrate surface with SAMs of $\gamma$ -CD provides a general and versatile method for the hetero-epitaxial growth of CD-MOFs with improved surface-attached nucleation and controllable orientation of the CD-MOF crystals on both flat and curved surfaces.
In order to investigate the electrochemical characteristics of the oriented surface-attached CD-MOF films, electrical devices were fabricated (Figure 3) by hetero-epitaxial growth of CD-MOFs on substrates with gold electrodes using the host-guest strategy, and were evaluated (Figure S8) as the electrochemical sensors for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The Corning Eagle $2000^{\mathrm{TM}}$ glass slides modified with pyrene groups were used (Figure 3a-c) as the starting substrates, on which the gold electrodes were evaporated (Figure 3b) with a designed pattern using a mask. Subsequently, the CD-MOF film was grown (Figure 3c) on the electrode-attached substrate with a similar protocol to that already described. The microscopic image shows (Figure 3d) that every electrode pair on the substrate has a channel with a width of ${\sim}100~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , filled with continuous polycrystalline film, which is evidence for the successful integration of CD-MOF film into an electrical device. The resulting devices were immersed into $n$ -hexane before measurement in order to fill the pores. The CD-MOF device was next placed in a semi-enclosed chamber under ambient air, and then its electrodes connected (Figure 3e, S9a) to a potentiostat using a two probe method. Upon applying a constant potential of $10\ \mathrm{V},$ the current was monitored when the CD-MOF device was exposed to air (relative humidity $=55\%$ ) or purged with a continuous $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas stream at a constant flow rate of $1\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{m}^{3}/\mathrm{min}$ . A stable baseline current was established with a value of ${\sim}10\ \mathrm{nA}$ upon exposure to air (Figure S9b), and the proton conductivity $(\upsigma)$ of the
CD-MOF film was calculated (Figure S10), based on the proton conductance, length and cross-sectional area of the conduction channel. In contrast, the suspension of CD-MOF crystalline powders in hexane was drop-coated onto the electrode substrates to fabricate (Figure S11a) the control device, which was demonstrated (Figure S11b) to be stacked crystals (Figure S11c) with random orientations by SEM. The baseline current of the control device was ${\sim}0.03$ nA (Figure S11d) upon exposing it to air, which is ${\sim}1/300$ of the value for the oriented CD-MOF film, indicating that the compact and ordered packing of crystals with out-of-plane ( $c$ -axial) orientations significantly improves the contact surface of the (100) lattice plane between crystallites parallel to the substrates with remarkable enhancement of intrinsic charge transporting. Furthermore, when using this platform in the gas sensing, the magnitude of the current was observed (Figure 3f) to undergo a sharp decrease to 1/50 of the baseline within 3 seconds upon exposure to a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas stream. The starting current level was recovered when the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ flow was removed by exposing the device to air. The reversible changes in current were reproducible over more than 4 cycles. In contrast to the pressed pellets of CD-MOF crystalline powders in our previous report,44 which exhibit ex situ reversible cycles of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas sorption in a sealed glass tube left for $5\mathrm{min}$ , followed by regeneration at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $40\mathrm{min}$ , the CD-MOF film reported here shows rapid response and in situ reversible cycles, which herald the practicability of potential applications for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ detection.
In summary, we have demonstrated a general host-guest strategy for the epitaxial growth of CD-MOFs on substrates, which provides, not only surface-attached CD-MOF polycrystalline films on flat substrates with an out-of-plane ( $c$ -axis) structural orientation, but also the core-shell structure with microspheres as the core and CD-MOFs as the shell. Using the host-guest strategy, the derivatization of the substrate surface with SAMs of $\gamma$ -CD has been achieved by means of host-guest complexation of the $\gamma$ -CD tori and pyrene groups covalently modified on the surface. The derivatized substrate surface has, not only led to the CD-MOF films on the substrate with $\mathrm{mm}^{2}$ -scale continuous areas by improving the surface-attached nucleation, but also led to oriented CD-MOF polycrystalline films by controlling the nucleation and direction of crystal growth. In addition, this host-guest strategy has been employed to integrate the oriented CD-MOF films into an electrical devices which not only demonstrates an enhanced proton conductance 300x greater than control devices prepared from CD-MOF crystalline powders, but also displays rapid and reversible response cycles upon exposure to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and ambient air alternatively. Finally, epitaxial growth of CD-MOFs using the present host-guest strategy, not only represents a significant addition to the toolbox for the fabrication of surface-attached MOF films and nanoarchitectures with core and MOF shells built around the metal nodes of $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ , but also provides the opportunity to develop wider potential applications of these green porous framework materials.
